{
  "dom_id": "step-q346718",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(2,1)$ and is concave down on the interval $(1,4).$ If ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=1,$ estimate the value of $f(1.6)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.$\\,f(1.6)\u2248$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{\u2032}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we have $f(2)=1.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =1+1(x-2) \\\\  & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.6) & \u2248L(1.6) \\\\  & =1.6-1 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(1,4),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=2,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{overestimate}.$",
